__d(function(e,a,o,t,s,i,r){s.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:1,chapter_title:"Food: Where Does It Come From?",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What are the two main sources of all food?",back:"All food comes from two sources: plants and animals. Every ingredient in every dish you eat can be traced back to either a plant (rice, dal, vegetables, fruits) or an animal (milk, eggs, meat, honey).",memory_hook:"P-A rule: Every food is either Plant or Animal. There is no third option in nature."},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name the six parts of a plant that are used as food, with one example each.",back:"Root (carrot), Stem (potato), Leaf (spinach), Flower (cauliflower), Fruit (mango), Seed (rice). Every part of a plant can potentially be eaten \u2014 we use all six parts as food sources.",memory_hook:"Remember 'RSLFFS': Root-Stem-Leaf-Flower-Fruit-Seed. Or: Really Silly Lions Frequently Feast on Seeds."},{id:"fc03",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a herbivore? Give three examples.",back:"A herbivore is an animal that eats only plants for its food. Examples include cows (eat grass and hay), deer (eat leaves and grass), elephants (eat bamboo and fruit), goats, and rabbits. Herbivores usually have flat teeth designed for grinding plant material.",memory_hook:"HERBI sounds like 'herbs' \u2014 a herbivore eats herbs and plants. Herb-ivore = plant eater."},{id:"fc04",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a carnivore? Give three examples.",back:"A carnivore is an animal that eats only the flesh of other animals for its food. Examples include lions, tigers, leopards, eagles, snakes, and sharks. Carnivores typically have sharp teeth and strong claws for catching and consuming prey.",memory_hook:"CARNI = carne = meat in Latin. Carnivore = meat eater. Think 'carnage' \u2014 associated with killing for food."},{id:"fc05",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is an omnivore? Are humans herbivores, carnivores, or omnivores?",back:"An omnivore is an animal that eats both plants and animals. Humans are omnivores \u2014 our digestive system can process both plant-based foods (rice, vegetables, fruits) and animal-based foods (milk, eggs, meat). Other omnivores include dogs, bears, crows, and cockroaches.",memory_hook:"OMNI = all (omnipresent = everywhere). Omnivore = eats ALL types of food \u2014 plants and animals."},{id:"fc06",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is nectar, and what do honey bees do with it?",back:"Nectar is a sweet liquid produced inside flowers to attract insects and birds for pollination. Honey bees collect nectar from flowers, carry it back to their hive, and process it into honey. Honey is therefore an animal product (made by bees) derived from a plant source (nectar from flowers).",memory_hook:"Nectar \u2192 Bee \u2192 Honey. The flower donates, the bee transforms, and we enjoy. Three steps to the world's sweetest food."},{id:"fc07",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is potato a root or a stem? Why does it matter?",back:"Potato is a stem \u2014 specifically a modified underground stem called a tuber. It is NOT a root. You can confirm this because potato has 'eyes' (buds) from which new plants grow \u2014 roots do not have buds. Carrot and radish are true roots. Getting this right is important for classification questions in exams.",memory_hook:"Potato has EYES (buds). Roots are blind \u2014 they have no buds. Potato = stemmy underground shop, not a root."},{id:"fc08",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Is a tomato a fruit or a vegetable? Explain the scientific answer.",back:"Scientifically, a tomato is a FRUIT. A fruit is defined as the part of a plant that develops from the flower and contains seeds. Tomatoes develop from the flower of the tomato plant and contain seeds \u2014 so they are fruits. In common cooking language, tomato is called a vegetable, but this is not the botanical (scientific) classification.",memory_hook:"FRUIT test: Does it have seeds? Does it grow from a flower? YES to both = it's a FRUIT. Tomato passes both tests."},{id:"fc09",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why are plants called 'producers' in a food chain?",back:"Plants are called producers because they can make (produce) their own food through photosynthesis \u2014 using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide. They do not need to eat other organisms to get energy. All animals (including humans) ultimately depend on plants for food, making plants the foundation of every food chain.",memory_hook:"Producers = factories. Green plant leaves are solar-powered food factories running 24/7 using sunlight as electricity."},{id:"fc10",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name four animal products we use as food (without killing the animal).",back:"Milk (from cows, buffaloes, goats), eggs (from hens and ducks), honey (made by bees), and silk (from silkworms \u2014 though silk itself is not food, the concept applies). These are products animals produce without the animal being killed, showing that not all animal-sourced food requires animal death.",memory_hook:"MEH + S: Milk, Eggs, Honey (and Silk) \u2014 the four gifts animals give without sacrificing their lives."},{id:"fc11",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why do different animals have different types of teeth?",back:"The type of teeth an animal has is directly related to its diet. Herbivores have broad, flat teeth (molars) for grinding plant material. Carnivores have sharp, pointed teeth (canines) for tearing meat. Omnivores like humans have both flat molars (for grinding plants) and canine teeth (for tearing), reflecting our ability to eat both types of food.",memory_hook:"Teeth tell the truth about diet. Flat teeth = plants, Sharp teeth = meat, Both = omnivore. Look in a mirror \u2014 you'll see both!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is ragi (finger millet) and why is it important in Karnataka?",back:"Ragi is a cereal grain (seed) that is the most important food crop in Karnataka. It is grown in districts like Tumakuru, Kolar, and Ramanagara, and is used to make ragi mudde (a nutritious ball eaten with sambar or saaru) and ragi roti. It is rich in calcium, iron, and fibre, making it highly nutritious. Karnataka is India's largest producer of ragi.",memory_hook:"Ragi = Karnataka's power grain. The mudde that built generations of Karnataka farmers \u2014 all from a tiny seed."},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How is honey both a plant-related and an animal-related food?",back:"Honey is classified as an animal product because it is made by honey bees (which are insects \u2014 a type of animal). However, honey's raw material \u2014 nectar \u2014 comes from flowers (a plant source). So honey has a dual connection: plant source (nectar from flowers) + animal process (bees collecting and converting nectar into honey in their hive).",memory_hook:"Honey = Plant's gift (nectar) + Bee's labour (animal). It takes both a flower and a bee to make one jar of honey."},{id:"fc14",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Name two examples each of: foods from roots, foods from leaves, foods from seeds.",back:"Roots: carrot and radish (also beetroot, turnip). Leaves: spinach and cabbage (also curry leaves, lettuce, fenugreek). Seeds: rice and wheat (also dal, groundnut, maize, sunflower seeds). Seeds are the most commonly eaten plant part worldwide.",memory_hook:"RLS = Roots go down (carrot, radish), Leaves go up (spinach, cabbage), Seeds start it all (rice, wheat)."},{id:"fc15",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Classify these animals as herbivore, carnivore, or omnivore: cow, crow, snake, dog, deer.",back:"Cow \u2014 Herbivore (eats only grass, hay, and leaves). Crow \u2014 Omnivore (eats seeds, insects, scraps, and small animals). Snake \u2014 Carnivore (eats frogs, rats, birds, and other small animals). Dog \u2014 Omnivore (eats meat, rice, and vegetables). Deer \u2014 Herbivore (eats only grass, leaves, and shrubs).",memory_hook:"Test: What does it eat? Only plants = H. Only animals = C. Both = O. Apply HCO like traffic lights: Green (plants), Red (meat), Orange (both)."},{id:"fc16",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a tuber? Name two examples of food tubers.",back:"A tuber is a thick, fleshy underground stem that stores food for the plant. Tubers have buds (eyes) from which new plants can grow. Examples of food tubers: potato (the most common) and sweet potato. Yam is another example. Tubers are stems, not roots \u2014 this is a common exam question.",memory_hook:"Tuber = Underground stem hotel. The plant stores its food reserves in the tuber \u2014 and we eat those reserves."},{id:"fc17",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is photosynthesis and how does it connect to our food?",back:"Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants make food (glucose/starch) using sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide. All food we eat ultimately comes from this process \u2014 plants use it to make their own food, and herbivores eat plants, and carnivores eat herbivores. Without photosynthesis, there would be no food on Earth.",memory_hook:"Photo = light, synthesis = making. Plants are solar chefs \u2014 they cook food using sunlight. Every meal you eat started as sunlight captured by a plant leaf."},{id:"fc18",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'edible' mean? Give an example of an edible and a non-edible part of the same plant.",back:"Edible means safe and fit to be eaten. Example from the mango plant: the mango fruit is edible (we eat it), but the mango leaves are non-edible (we do not eat them). Similarly, in a tomato plant, the fruit is edible but the leaves and stems contain toxins and are non-edible.",memory_hook:"Edible = E-dible = Eat-able. Not all parts of a plant are edible \u2014 knowing which part is safe is important for survival."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A traditional Karnataka meal has ragi mudde, saaru (rasam with tomato and dal), and buttermilk. Classify each ingredient as plant or animal source.",back:"Ragi mudde \u2014 ragi (seed) = plant source. Saaru \u2014 toor dal (seed), tomato (fruit), tamarind (fruit), spices (seeds/fruits) = plant sources. Buttermilk \u2014 made from curd (from milk, from a cow) = animal source. Conclusion: This meal is predominantly plant-based with one animal-sourced component (buttermilk).",memory_hook:"Traditional Karnataka meals = mostly seeds + fruits from plants. The animal source (milk/buttermilk) is the supporting actor."},{id:"fc20",type:"Application",difficulty:"Easy",front:"A lion eats a deer. The deer had eaten grass. What does this tell you about energy flow in a food chain?",back:"This is a simple food chain: Grass (plant/producer) \u2192 Deer (herbivore/primary consumer) \u2192 Lion (carnivore/secondary consumer). Energy flows from the sun (captured by grass through photosynthesis) to the deer (which eats grass) to the lion (which eats the deer). All energy in the chain originally came from the sun via a plant.",memory_hook:"Food chain = energy relay race. Sun passes to plant, plant passes to herbivore, herbivore passes to carnivore. The sun started the race."}]}},2896,[]);